{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":5911859,"ns":0,"title":"Ax–Kochen theorem","extract":"The Ax–Kochen theorem, named for James Ax and Simon B. Kochen, states that for each positive integer d there is a finite set Yd of prime numbers, such that if p is any prime not in Yd then every homogeneous polynomial of degree d over the p-adic numbers in at least d2 + 1 variables has a nontrivial zero.","description":"On the existence of zeros of homogeneous polynomials over the p-adic numbers","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}